ost 4 of the 6 points. So it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines anchored at column 2 points. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=5. This matches our earlier conclusion.\n\nNow for n=6, we have rightmost 4 columns: columns 3,4,5,6 with sizes 4,3,2,1. We have 4 sunny lines, each anchored at a point in column 3 (size 4). We need to cover column 4 (3 points), column 5 (2 points), column 6 (1 point). That's 6 points to cover with 4 lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column, so across 4 lines we can cover at most 4 points in column 4, which is enough (since column 4 has 3 points). Similarly, column 5 has 2 points, we can cover them with 2 of the lines. Column 6 has 1 point, we can cover with one line. So in principle it's possible. However, we need to ensure that each line anchored at a column 3 point can be chosen to also pass through a point in column 4, column 5, column 6 as needed, and that the assignments are consistent (i.e., each line can cover at most one point per column). This is similar to a matching problem. We need to see if there exists a set of 4 lines anchored at the 4 points in column 3 that collectively cover all points in columns 4-6. Let's attempt to find such lines.\n\nColumn 3 points: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4). Column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). Column 5 points: (5,1),(5,2). Column 6 point: (6,1).\n\nWe need to assign each anchor a line that passes through one point in each of columns 4,5,6 (maybe not all). Since we have 4 anchors, we can assign each anchor to cover a distinct point in column 4 (3 points) and one anchor to cover the remaining column 4 point? Actually we have 3 points in column 4, so we need at least 3 lines to cover them. We have 4 lines, so one line may not cover a column 4 point. Similarly, we have 2 points in column 5, need at least 2 lines to cover them. We have 4 lines, so okay. Column 6 has 1 point, need at least 1 line.\n\nThus we need to assign each anchor to cover at most one point in each column,